From 4b52dea7a8fa6a9b3ef6a381e1b19ae937a53fb6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Timur Ravilov <93673987+t0b1kent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:03:38 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 18/19] DIV/IDIV: STATUS_INTEGER_OVERFLOW for a quotient
 overflow (MACRUNNER_FEX_DIV_OVERFLOW_DE)

With MACRUNNER_FEX_DIV_OVERFLOW_DE=1 (patch 0014) a DIV/IDIV quotient overflow raises #DE, but the
Windows frontend reported every #DE as EXCEPTION_INT_DIVIDE_BY_ZERO (0xC0000094). Under Prism
(Windows 11 on Arm 26200, emulator 10.0.26100.9168) a quotient overflow with a non-zero divisor is
EXCEPTION_INT_OVERFLOW (0xC0000095), and a zero divisor is 0xC0000094 even when the quotient would
also overflow; the same in x64 and WOW64 processes, 8 to 64 bits, at the dividing instruction.

Under the switch the fault block now tells the two apart: it reads the divisor operand again and
raises #DE with ErrorRegister 0 for a zero divisor and 1 otherwise, and
Source/Windows/Common/Exception.h reports err_code 1 as EXCEPTION_INT_OVERFLOW (ARM64EC and WOW64
share it). For a memory operand this is a second read, made only when the division faults.
Without the switch nothing changes: the zero-divisor check of patch 0011 raises #DE with
ErrorRegister 0 as before.

HB<->FEX oracle, native runner with 0017, arms OVERFLOW_DE and OVERFLOW_DE + PROVEN_HIGH: the 133
idiom cases in product and native mode and the 72 219 cases of the other corpora in product mode
give the same results as the series before this patch (the 2 fexici differences are RDTSC). The
oracle compares fault class and address, not the Windows code.

